        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix: preserve prompt cache continuity in embedded sessions",
        "body": "Related: #102175\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nLong-lived embedded sessions could lose prompt-cache continuity in three connected ways:\n\n- ambient `room_event` turns changed cache-visible delivery instructions and owner-only tool schemas;\n- preflight, manual, and queued compaction rebuilt prompt/tool state from the current turn's delivery mode instead of the session-stable prompt mode;\n- the built-in authenticated session stream was mistaken for a custom stream, so `openai-chatgpt-responses` runs used the managed full-context SSE adapter instead of OpenClaw's native cached WebSocket continuation path.\n\n## Fix\n\n- Separate effective source delivery from the session-stable prompt-facing delivery mode. Room events still require `message_tool_only` delivery without rewriting the cached prompt contract.\n- Keep the safe room-event owner-only core-tool inventory stable across sender changes while preserving execution authorization.\n- Carry the prompt-facing mode through primary runs, queued followups, Codex app-server prompt/tool builders, and every compaction entry point; compaction now rebuilds tools and system text from that stable mode.\n- Mark OpenClaw's built-in session auth wrapper and allow only that known wrapper to select the native `openai-chatgpt-responses` stream. Unmarked custom/provider streams retain their existing routing.\n\nThe native routing check is API-based, so it covers GPT-5.5 and GPT-5.6 without model-version branches.\n\n## Evidence\n\nRedacted same-session traces from the reported Discord room-event flow showed tool inventory flipping between 44 and 47 tools and cache reads collapsing to roughly 2k-10k or ceilinging around 37k-40k. With the room-event prompt/tool fix installed, warm turns with stable inventory reached roughly 49k-52k cached tokens on approximately 51k-55k context.\n\nThe route regression test reproduces the built-in auth-wrapper misclassification. A two-turn GPT-5.6 Sol WebSocket test then proves that the native provider sends the second turn with `previous_response_id` and only the new input, rather than replaying the full context.\n\n## Validation\n\nValidated at head `2ac9fefa3d684095460cffe0c97eca546a0fbd09` on upstream `main` `8dedb0ebcaef2bbe6dcd7b9ad137554b62ac1d21`:\n\n- focused regression suite: 18 project test files, 1,035 tests passed;\n- `pnpm tsgo:core`, `pnpm tsgo:core:test`, and `pnpm tsgo:test:packages` passed;\n- all 37 changed files passed `oxfmt --check` and `oxlint`;\n- `git diff --check origin/main...HEAD` passed.\n\n## Boundaries\n\n- Effective room-event delivery remains strict; this does not make private final text visible.\n- Non-owner execution of owner-only tools remains blocked.\n- Custom provider/session streams are not rerouted.\n- Native `auto` transport may still fall back to SSE after a real WebSocket failure; this change makes the native continuation path reachable and tested, but does not claim every upstream request will be cached.\n\nDisclaimer: This PR was filed and updated autonomously by Codex on behalf of the reporter. Private user, channel, host, credential, and full trace identifiers were removed from the evidence.",
